WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.126
Hey, guys. How's it going?

00:00:01.126 --> 00:00:02.335
So today I want to walk you

00:00:02.335 --> 00:00:06.047
through the architectural design process
from start to finish, and

00:00:06.339 --> 00:00:09.718
you can get a little sense of how we work
with other professionals in the process.

00:00:09.884 --> 00:00:10.635
If you're new here,

00:00:10.635 --> 00:00:14.889
my name is Dami and I am an architect
in beautiful Vancouver, B.C..

00:00:15.306 --> 00:00:18.768
On this channel we talk about architecture
and design, as well as some tools

00:00:18.768 --> 00:00:19.602
and strategies

00:00:19.602 --> 00:00:23.565
that can help us have more meaningful
and fulfilling careers in architecture.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:25.775
This video is sponsored by Autodesk.

00:00:25.775 --> 00:00:29.112
To get started with your subscription,
click on the link in the description.

00:00:31.406 --> 00:00:32.365
So this was a project

00:00:32.365 --> 00:00:35.827
we did for a net zero
competition called Race zero,

00:00:35.827 --> 00:00:40.415
and our team actually won first place
in the multifamily housing category.

00:00:40.623 --> 00:00:45.045
It was a multidisciplinary team
with myself and two architecture students,

00:00:45.045 --> 00:00:49.632
a building science team, and the developer
who had already purchased the site.

00:00:49.716 --> 00:00:52.844
Every client is going to come to you
with different project goals.

00:00:52.844 --> 00:00:58.391
It can be a simple, functional goal,
or maybe to maximize views or

00:00:58.391 --> 00:01:03.480
an energy target or improving
the existing conditions, whatever it is.

00:01:03.521 --> 00:01:06.983
It's very important
to understand this early on

00:01:07.275 --> 00:01:12.072
and let this guide the process,
especially for sustainability targets,

00:01:12.072 --> 00:01:15.784
whether it's LEED or net zero
or Passive House

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:18.536
or just like future proofing the building.

00:01:18.703 --> 00:01:21.915
These will have a pretty big impact
on the building.

00:01:22.499 --> 00:01:25.877
So for projects like this, it's
very important to get the consultants

00:01:25.877 --> 00:01:32.801
involved earlier on in the process
for an integrated design process.

00:01:33.009 --> 00:01:35.845
Before starting any design,
we need to analyze the site

00:01:35.845 --> 00:01:39.557
to set out the constraints
that our design is going to respond to.

00:01:40.058 --> 00:01:43.645
So you're going to be looking at all
the existing conditions, like

00:01:43.645 --> 00:01:49.901
the neighboring buildings, the topography,
as well as potential future conditions.

00:01:50.026 --> 00:01:53.530
And you also want to take a look
at the zoning regulations

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:57.826
and the maximum buildable area
or setbacks, the FSR.

00:01:58.326 --> 00:02:01.246
And actually,
if you want a detailed breakdown

00:02:01.246 --> 00:02:04.707
of how we develop a massing model
from the zoning requirements,

00:02:04.999 --> 00:02:07.127
check out the video right here.

00:02:07.127 --> 00:02:11.381
One of the most challenging
and contentious things

00:02:11.381 --> 00:02:15.552
about building on
urban sites is the parking.

00:02:15.885 --> 00:02:18.847
Most cities have minimum
parking requirements

00:02:18.847 --> 00:02:22.267
because they don't want their streets
to be littered with cars.

00:02:22.725 --> 00:02:25.186
But parking is expensive

00:02:25.436 --> 00:02:27.856
and it takes up a lot of space.

00:02:28.439 --> 00:02:31.484
And in this construction climate,

00:02:31.484 --> 00:02:34.821
it can sometimes make or break a project.

00:02:35.238 --> 00:02:39.617
So you just want to check to see
if there's any parking relaxations

00:02:39.617 --> 00:02:42.745
for your site,
which is, you know, typically dependent

00:02:42.745 --> 00:02:47.292
on your site's proximity
to transit or other amenities.

00:02:47.292 --> 00:02:50.837
As soon as I have the site info,
I start modeling things in Revit

00:02:50.837 --> 00:02:54.674
pretty early on just to understand
the relationship to the site

00:02:54.674 --> 00:02:59.804
and develop some rough
massing options for the building.

00:03:00.096 --> 00:03:03.933
These massing models
now become the basis for your site

00:03:03.933 --> 00:03:07.896
strategy, which you develop
through a process called Diagraming.

00:03:07.896 --> 00:03:10.481
In these early schematic stages.

00:03:10.481 --> 00:03:13.526
We iterate
through a bunch of different diagrams

00:03:13.693 --> 00:03:17.238
to help us have more high level

00:03:17.238 --> 00:03:20.742
discussion
on the direction of our design strategy.

00:03:21.117 --> 00:03:24.120
Without going too much into detail,
this process can help

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:28.124
you figure out the relationship
between your building and the site

00:03:28.124 --> 00:03:31.753
or the building circulation
or the building's

00:03:31.753 --> 00:03:35.215
organizational strategy.

00:03:35.423 --> 00:03:39.719
So through the process of Diagraming,
you'll probably flush out

00:03:39.719 --> 00:03:45.808
a lot of the bad ideas and select
a few ones that you or your team likes.

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:49.812
You can definitely have your favorites,
but you still want to try

00:03:49.812 --> 00:03:53.274
to present
all of the viable options to the client.

00:03:53.274 --> 00:03:57.487
And I think the client always appreciates
being a part of the decision

00:03:57.487 --> 00:03:58.905
making process.

00:03:58.905 --> 00:04:03.159
As you present these massing options,
you always want back it up with numbers,

00:04:04.118 --> 00:04:04.827
whether it's

00:04:04.827 --> 00:04:08.623
a developer or a family
wanting to build their first home.

00:04:08.873 --> 00:04:13.753
If the math doesn't work out, chances are
the project isn't going to go through.

00:04:14.045 --> 00:04:17.173
So you want to be really clear on

00:04:17.173 --> 00:04:20.593
how much area
you're providing with each option.

00:04:21.010 --> 00:04:24.681
One of the great things
about using a big modeling software

00:04:24.681 --> 00:04:28.434
like Revit
is that you can do these massive studies

00:04:28.434 --> 00:04:32.855
and the models going to give you
a pretty accurate breakdown of the areas

00:04:32.855 --> 00:04:36.025
without you
actually having to calculate it manually.

00:04:36.067 --> 00:04:40.363
The clients are going to be running
their own numbers to make the projects

00:04:40.571 --> 00:04:45.326
make sense financially,
but you are responsible for showing them

00:04:45.326 --> 00:04:50.123
the areas and the quantity and the quality
of the spaces that you're creating.

00:04:50.415 --> 00:04:53.501
There's usually a bit of back and forth

00:04:53.501 --> 00:04:58.381
with the client at the stage,
especially if the client's a developer.

00:04:58.381 --> 00:05:01.634
They're going to want to maximize
the profits on this building,

00:05:01.843 --> 00:05:06.097
meaning they're going to want to maximize
on the number of units in most cases.

00:05:06.222 --> 00:05:11.394
So you need to remind them
of the project's goals from time to time.

00:05:11.436 --> 00:05:15.481
In this case, for this project,
the most efficient unit layout

00:05:15.481 --> 00:05:19.527
would have been to have units
on the South End and on the North End.

00:05:20.153 --> 00:05:24.824
But one of the main goals for the project
was to reach net zero

00:05:24.824 --> 00:05:29.287
or Passive House Standard,
and the building science team identified

00:05:29.495 --> 00:05:33.416
pretty early on that
if we wanted to aim for that target,

00:05:33.416 --> 00:05:37.086
we would need to use passive
heating and cooling strategies,

00:05:37.628 --> 00:05:40.965
meaning we'd need to orient the building
north, south.

00:05:41.174 --> 00:05:45.678
This way, every unit can take advantage
of the solar working from the south side

00:05:45.678 --> 00:05:48.097
with passive ventilation
through the building.

00:05:48.348 --> 00:05:52.143
So there was quite a lot of back
and forth during this process

00:05:52.143 --> 00:05:55.355
because the premium you can charge
for being a net zero building

00:05:55.355 --> 00:05:59.025
is not quite enough
to make up for an entire unit.

00:05:59.192 --> 00:06:01.903
That's why these types of discussions
on the project's

00:06:01.903 --> 00:06:07.325
goals are really important
to have early on.

00:06:07.492 --> 00:06:11.788
Once you figure out the general direction
of the building, you start to fine tune

00:06:11.788 --> 00:06:15.625
the layout with furniture,
blocks or doors or windows.

00:06:15.625 --> 00:06:20.254
I usually print them out
and overlay them with sketch, or

00:06:20.296 --> 00:06:24.675
I bring the base drawings into Procreate
and start sketching directly on there.

00:06:24.759 --> 00:06:28.513
Once I got the layout on sketch,
I bring it back into Revit

00:06:28.721 --> 00:06:33.851
and the process usually goes back
and forth between manual and digital.

00:06:33.851 --> 00:06:36.020
You might have guessed by now
drafting and 3D

00:06:36.020 --> 00:06:39.941
modeling is a pretty integral
part of my design process.

00:06:39.941 --> 00:06:43.945
Right from the beginning, I've always used
Autodesk products like AutoCAD

00:06:43.945 --> 00:06:48.449
and riveted by just this past year,
thanks to some of the collaborations

00:06:48.449 --> 00:06:52.036
that we wouldn't doing, I've been learning
a lot more about the other

00:06:52.036 --> 00:06:55.206
creative industries
that Autodesk is involved in.

00:06:55.456 --> 00:06:58.960
They're not just leaders
in the architecture or construction

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:04.006
industries, but also in manufacturing,
media, entertainment.

00:07:04.006 --> 00:07:05.967
And they have this goal of empowering

00:07:05.967 --> 00:07:09.846
innovation everywhere
to solve challenges of all scales.

00:07:09.887 --> 00:07:13.599
If you want to learn more about some of
the innovative projects they're part of,

00:07:14.058 --> 00:07:15.101
I'm going to lead the link

00:07:15.101 --> 00:07:19.188
to my visit to the Autodesk Gallery
in San Francisco right here.

00:07:19.480 --> 00:07:22.442
Revit is a building information
modeling software

00:07:22.442 --> 00:07:25.736
with the ability
to generate things like area calculation

00:07:25.736 --> 00:07:30.241
pins or windows schedules
or draw schedules directly from the model.

00:07:30.283 --> 00:07:34.328
It automates a lot of the things
that historically architects

00:07:34.328 --> 00:07:35.913
would have had to do manually.

00:07:35.913 --> 00:07:40.126
I personally think in order
for architecture to move into the future,

00:07:40.418 --> 00:07:43.963
we need to automate more and more things

00:07:44.046 --> 00:07:49.760
so that we can focus on important things
like designing and working with clients.

00:07:49.844 --> 00:07:53.681
Honestly, I can't imagine Revit
not being a part of my workflow.

00:07:53.806 --> 00:07:56.809
If you're a student,
they're financing options, meaning

00:07:56.809 --> 00:07:59.854
with a valid education
email, you can use a product for free.

00:07:59.896 --> 00:08:04.442
They also have tons of free tutorials
and resources to help you get started.

00:08:04.609 --> 00:08:10.239
To get started with your subscription,
click on the link in the description.

00:08:10.239 --> 00:08:12.909
As architects we try to create order

00:08:13.326 --> 00:08:18.080
and that goes for elevations as well
for this project once again,

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:22.001
because we did have this net zero target,
the building science team

00:08:22.001 --> 00:08:26.797
gave us the maximum area of windows
that we could have on both facades.

00:08:27.048 --> 00:08:33.095
We also had requirements for overhangs
with a fairly strict depth.

00:08:33.304 --> 00:08:35.765
So working within these two constraints,

00:08:36.098 --> 00:08:38.768
you know,
as well as the interior functions,

00:08:39.101 --> 00:08:43.606
you start to develop the elevation trees
is probably the best tool

00:08:43.606 --> 00:08:47.818
for developing rough ideas
because no idea is too precious.

00:08:47.818 --> 00:08:50.404
And you can layer ideas
one on top of another.

00:08:50.488 --> 00:08:54.700
You can start to develop the elevation
by deciding what areas to highlight,

00:08:54.909 --> 00:08:59.247
use proportions or the golden section
to break up the facade.

00:08:59.247 --> 00:09:01.791
Just like you do with the plans,
you start diagrammatic clean

00:09:01.999 --> 00:09:05.962
and then you start to build in details
or ideas for materials.

00:09:06.128 --> 00:09:09.006
Once I've got the idea,
I bring it back into

00:09:09.006 --> 00:09:12.468
Revit to get a better sense
of how it works in 3D.

00:09:12.468 --> 00:09:16.097
And once I model that in 3D,
I do another printout

00:09:16.097 --> 00:09:19.308
of these different views
and I just keep developing

00:09:19.308 --> 00:09:23.145
more and more iterations,
either on paper or on my iPad.

00:09:23.354 --> 00:09:27.650
And it's a workflow
that seems to work for me.

00:09:27.650 --> 00:09:30.987
You know that saying a picture is worth
a thousand words

00:09:31.487 --> 00:09:35.199
renderings are a very important
part of the process because

00:09:35.199 --> 00:09:38.536
no matter how good of an explanation
you have your project,

00:09:38.953 --> 00:09:42.164
if you can't inspire them
about how this could be,

00:09:42.582 --> 00:09:46.085
they're not going to shell out
millions of dollars to build this thing.

00:09:46.669 --> 00:09:49.589
The rendering is the thing
that sells the project

00:09:49.589 --> 00:09:52.466
to the client
or the city or the stakeholders.

00:09:52.842 --> 00:09:56.554
So we typically develop
multiple renderings

00:09:56.554 --> 00:09:58.514
throughout the lifespan of the project.

00:09:58.514 --> 00:10:02.351
We do it during schematic design
and as we develop the design

00:10:02.351 --> 00:10:06.522
and everyone gets have feedback
on the design like the client

00:10:06.522 --> 00:10:08.024
or the city or the community.

00:10:08.024 --> 00:10:11.319
And through this you refine and develop
the design even more.

00:10:11.444 --> 00:10:17.033
Now this covers the architectural design
process in a very, very broad strokes,

00:10:17.116 --> 00:10:22.455
and I haven't even touched on how
your consultants could impact the design.

00:10:22.455 --> 00:10:26.417
If you're wondering about
how we actually work with consultants

00:10:26.417 --> 00:10:29.295
and the role of each of the major
engineers,

